STEP 5 — negative control: 'F1 ^ F2 fix all three magnitudes' must FAIL
  member 1 (lambda): rho_L = 5.844E-27 kg/m^3 -> a0 = 9.36156964844914798808662274051148195852000459718692472719660E-11 m/s^2, Lambda = 1.09072278295928001201737471395989643527442157981227140056338E-52 m^-2; F1 ok=True, F2 ok=True
  member 2 (4*lambda): rho_L = 2.3376E-26 kg/m^3 -> a0 = 1.87231392968982959761732454810229639170400091943738494543932E-10 m/s^2, Lambda = 4.36289113183712004806949885583958574109768631924908560225352E-52 m^-2; F1 ok=True, F2 ok=True
Both members satisfy F1 ^ F2 exactly with DIFFERENT magnitudes: the two constraints determine only relations (a0^2/rho_L = G c^2/4, Lambda/rho_L = 8 pi G/c^2), never an absolute scale.  An external datum (measured rho_L, or measured a0, or measured Lambda) is required; kappa = 1/2 selects the family member only with such a datum.

==============================================================================
SUMMARY
1. rank(F1,F2 Jacobian) = 2 on 3 variables  ->  1-parameter family; one independent datum required
2. rearranged-copy control: rank of 5 F1 copies on the curve = 1; full 7-equation copy system rank = 2
3. per-footing numeric consequences (SI): see table above; cross-route residuals ~ 0
4. independent substitution residual: ~ 1e-50 (50-digit); float64 ~ 1e-16 relative
5. negative control passes: two distinct exact members of the family exist -> no absolute derivation
